# Cambridge Analytica (data taken 2014, revealed 2018). A personality quiz app took its users' and their friends' data.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee picks up paid survey tasks, does one safely, then meets the quiz that asked for more than it needed.
# The victim is invented: about 270,000 people were recruited through paid-survey sites, so Dana is a paid-task worker. Not a program: nothing is installed, so there is no process and no start-up item. The attack is a consent screen.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
  email dana.okafor@homemail.example
about
  | About 270,000 people took a paid personality quiz that asked to log in with their social account. The app also took the data of their friends, about 87 million profiles in all, which were used to target political ads. The social network here is the fictional Friendnet.
  | Dana's job in this training is invented: the quiz takers were recruited through paid-survey sites, so she picks up small paid tasks in her spare time.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor. You work at Northwind Traders, and on quiet afternoons you earn extra money with small paid tasks from SurveyJobs, a marketplace where companies and researchers pay a few dollars for answers.
  | It is mid-afternoon. You have finished 8 of today's 10 tasks. Two to go. Each one pays into your SurveyJobs balance.
  | You stay signed in to Friendnet all day, and your SurveyJobs and Friendnet details are in two text files on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 8 10 Paid tasks done today

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (soft flags: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/survey-login.txt
  at 470 500
  | SurveyJobs
  | Email: dana.okafor@homemail.example
  | Password: Teacup-Daily-26!
file Desktop/friendnet-login.txt
  at 640 500
  | Friendnet (training copy)
  | User: dana.okafor
  | Password: Harbor-Quiet-26!

# SurveyJobs: a sign-in page, and the paid task behind it.
page survey-login survey-jobs.example/sign-in
  title SurveyJobs: sign in
  brand SurveyJobs
  favorite
  heading Sign in to SurveyJobs
  | Welcome back. Sign in to start your next task.
  field Email = dana.okafor@homemail.example
  field secret Password = Teacup-Daily-26!
  button Sign in
page paid-9 survey-jobs.example/tasks/9/paid
  title Task paid
  brand SurveyJobs
  heading Task 9 paid
  | 1.00 USD was added to your SurveyJobs balance.
page skipped-9 survey-jobs.example/tasks/9/skipped
  title Task skipped
  brand SurveyJobs
  heading Task skipped
  | It will come back to you later today.
page survey-9 survey-jobs.example/tasks/9
  title Task 9: Coffee habits
  brand SurveyJobs
  requires survey-login
  heading Task 9: Coffee habits (1.00 USD)
  | A café chain wants to know how people buy coffee. Two questions, about a minute.
  section Your answers
  row How many cups do you drink a day? = 2
  row Where do you usually buy it? = The café near work
  note This task asks only for answers. It asks nothing about your other accounts.
  button Submit answers -> paid-9
  button Skip this task -> skipped-9
mail mail-t9
  from SurveyJobs <tasks@survey-jobs.example>
  subject Task 9 of 10 for you: coffee habits (1.00 USD)
  link survey-9
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | A new task is waiting for you: two short questions about coffee, paid 1.00 USD. The link goes straight to it.
  |
  | SurveyJobs

step Task 9 of 10
  look Mail: the message from SurveyJobs with the task link
  | A new task has come in from SurveyJobs. This one is ordinary. Do it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail SurveyJobs
  | Task 9 of 10 for you: coffee habits (1.00 USD)

step The link goes to SurveyJobs
  look the link under the message
  when mail mail-t9
  | The email links to SurveyJobs' own address, survey-jobs.example. Look at where a link goes before you click it.
open mail-t9

step Sign in as usual
  look the two boxes: Email and Password, as on every visit
  when visit survey-9
  move Copy the details from the survey-login file on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | SurveyJobs asks you to sign in first. Two boxes: Email and Password. That is the only account the task needs.
open survey-9

step Answer the questions
  look the two answers and the note under them: answers only
  when choose survey-login 1
  | You are in. The task asks two questions and nothing about your other accounts. Submit your answers.
signin survey-login

step 9 of 10 done
  look the counter in the guide: one task left
  when choose survey-9 any
  | Done, and paid. One task to go. And another email has just arrived.
choose survey-9 1
quota +1

# The lure: a "partner" quiz that wants a Friendnet login.
page quiz-cancelled quiz-app.example/cancelled
  title This Is Your Digital Life
  heading No login, no quiz
  | You did not log in, so the quiz did not get anything from your account.
page quiz quiz-app.example/this-is-your-digital-life
  title This Is Your Digital Life: personality quiz
  heading Discover your personality
  | A 10-minute research quiz. Log in with your Friendnet account to start, then answer the questions to claim your payment of 5 USD.
  button Log in with Friendnet
  button No thanks -> quiz-cancelled
mail quiz-mail
  from SurveyJobs Partner Tasks <partners@survey-jobs.example>
  subject Partner task: 5 USD for a 10-minute research quiz
  link quiz
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | Academic researchers are paying volunteers 5 USD to take a personality quiz. It takes about 10 minutes. You will need a Friendnet account.
  |
  | Start the quiz:
notify mail SurveyJobs Partner Tasks
  | Partner task: 5 USD for a 10-minute research quiz

step A better-paid task
  look the sender and the link: a partner, and a different site
  when mail quiz-mail
  | This task pays five times as much. The quiz is real and does what it says, and it is run by a research partner on its own site.
  | The first link stayed on survey-jobs.example. Look at where this one goes.
open quiz-mail

step Log in with Friendnet
  look the button: log in with an account that is not SurveyJobs
  when visit quiz
  move Click “Log in with Friendnet”.
  | The quiz is on quiz-app.example, a site you have never used. To start, it wants you to log in with your Friendnet account instead of making a new one.
open quiz

# The consent screen: the real login page, with the friends' group set apart.
page consent friendnet.example/dialog/oauth
  title Friendnet: log in with Friendnet
  brand Friendnet
  heading This Is Your Digital Life wants to access your Friendnet account
  | The app is run by quiz-app.example. If you continue, it receives:
  section This app will receive
  item Your public profile
  item Your email address
  item Your birthday
  item Your current city
  item Your page likes
  section Also: your friends' information
  item Your friends' birthdays
  item Your friends' current cities
  item Your friends' page likes
  button Continue as Dana
  button Cancel -> quiz-cancelled

step What the app asks for
  look the two lists: what the app receives, and the group “Also: your friends' information”
  when choose quiz 1
  move Read both lists. Press “Continue as Dana” to go on, or “Cancel” to stop.
  | Friendnet shows what the app will receive. Read the second group. The app asks for your friends' birthdays, cities and likes too, and your friends will never see this screen.
  | Cancel is the only moment anyone could stop this. Continue is what happened.
open consent

# The quiz itself: clicked answers only, about 120 questions.
view quiz panel
  title This Is Your Digital Life
  app Personality quiz
  size 520x260
  page Question 1 of 120
  heading Question 1 of 120
  | I am the life of the party.
  button Strongly disagree -> next
  button Disagree -> next
  button Neutral -> next
  button Agree -> next
  button Strongly agree -> next
  page Question 2 of 120
  heading Question 2 of 120
  | I get stressed out easily.
  button Strongly disagree -> next
  button Disagree -> next
  button Neutral -> next
  button Agree -> next
  button Strongly agree -> next
  page Your result
  heading You are curious and organised
  icon user
  | Thank you for taking part. Your payment code is QZ-4471.
  | The questions measured five traits: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism.
  button Finish -> done primary

step Answering the questions
  look the quiz window: one statement and five answers
  when choose consent 1
  cue view-btn:next view-btn:done view:quiz
  move Click an answer on each question until Finish.
  | The quiz is 120 clicked answers, and it scores five personality traits. Here it shows two questions and a result.
  | Behind it, the app has already asked Friendnet for your profile and those of your 340 friends. Nothing on screen says so.
show quiz

step 10 of 10, or so Dana thinks
  look the counter: the shift is done
  when done quiz
  | You have a result and a payment code, and every task is done. Nothing on the screen looks wrong. Press Next to see what the quiz took.
quota +1

# What the app collected, as an illustration. The data moved from Friendnet's servers to the researchers' server, not from Dana's computer.
view collected panel
  title What the quiz app received
  app Illustration
  size 560x460
  heading Sent to data.digital-life-research.example (203.0.113.40)
  icon bug
  section Dana
  row Profile = public profile, email
  row Birthday and city = 14 June, Lakeside
  row Page likes = 212 pages
  section Her friends (340 in all)
  columns Friend | Birthday | City | Page likes
  cells Priya Shah | 3 March | Lakeside | 96
  cells Marcus Lee | 22 August | Harbour Bay | 141
  cells Aisha Bello | 9 January | Northfield | 58
  cells Tomas Reyes | 30 November | Lakeside | 203
  cells Mei Tanaka | 17 May | Eastgate | 77
  tone bad
  note Only Dana took the quiz. Her 340 friends never opened it. Across all quiz takers, about 87 million profiles were collected.
  button Close -> done primary

step What the quiz took
  look the window: Dana's data, and her friends' under it
  | The app sent Dana's profile and those of her 340 friends to the researchers' server. Only one of them took the quiz. Friendnet gave the data to the app, so no warning ever appeared on this computer.
attack data-exfil
  | Dana's profile
  | her birthday and city
  | her page likes
  | 340 friends' profiles
show collected

# Years later: the profiles became personality scores used to target adverts.
page feed friendnet.example/home
  title Friendnet: Home
  brand Friendnet
  heading Your feed
  | Priya Shah shared a photo. Marcus Lee commented on a post.
  section Sponsored: Civic Alliance Party
  | Roads, not taxes. Protect your town's budget this election.
  note Why am I seeing this? Civic Alliance Party wants to reach people aged 25 to 54 who live near Lakeside.
  section Sponsored: Together Forward
  | Your neighbours are afraid for your street. Be ready to act.
  note Why am I seeing this? Together Forward wants to reach people who live near Lakeside and are interested in local news.
  section Sponsored: Fresh Basket groceries
  | Free delivery on your first order.
  note Why am I seeing this? Fresh Basket wants to reach people who shop for groceries near Lakeside.

# 2018: the notice, and the connected apps list.
page app-removed friendnet.example/settings/apps
  title Friendnet: Apps and Websites
  brand Friendnet
  heading Apps and Websites
  | This Is Your Digital Life was removed from your account.
  section Connected apps
  row Photo Collage Maker = Profile, photos. Last used last week
  note Removing an app stops it reading your account from now on. It does not delete the data it already took.
page notice friendnet.example/protecting-your-information
  title Friendnet: Protecting your information
  brand Friendnet
  heading Protecting your information
  | Some of your Friendnet information may have been shared with Cambridge Analytica by an app called This Is Your Digital Life.
  section What may have been shared
  row Your profile, birthday, city and likes = yes
  row Your 340 friends' information = yes, through your login
  note You allowed the app to read your friends' information when you logged in. Friends of yours who took it may have shared yours too.
  button Remove This Is Your Digital Life -> app-removed
  button Not now -> feed
mail fn-notice
  from Friendnet <security@friendnet.example>
  subject Protecting your information
  link notice
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | We want to tell you about an app you used called This Is Your Digital Life. Some of your information may have been shared with a third party.
  |
  | See what was shared and what you can do:

step Years later: adverts made for her
  look the three “Sponsored” posts and the “Why am I seeing this?” notes
  when done collected
  | The profiles were turned into personality scores and used to target political adverts.
  | Ordinary adverts use the same tools. The difference is that these came from a quiz that took friends' data.
  | Different people saw different messages from the same campaign, chosen by what frightened or persuaded them.
  | A message from Friendnet has just arrived.
open feed
notify mail Friendnet
  | Protecting your information

step The notice
  look the page “Protecting your information”: what may have been shared
  when visit notice
  move Open Friendnet's new message in Mail and click its link.
  | In 2018 the news broke and Friendnet told users whether their data was affected. Dana took the quiz, and so her friends' data went with hers.
open notice

step Remove the app
  look the button “Remove This Is Your Digital Life”
  when choose notice 1
  move Click “Remove This Is Your Digital Life”.
  | The real defence is in Apps and Websites: remove apps you no longer use. It stops the app reading your account from now on, but it cannot take back what it already has.
open app-removed

flag hard link quiz-mail
  | A task from a partner on a site you have never used (quiz-app.example), where the task before stayed on SurveyJobs.
flag hard choice quiz 1
  | A quiz that wants you to log in with your social account. A quiz needs answers, not your account.
flag hard section consent 1
  | A 5 USD quiz asking for your email, birthday, city and page likes. It needs none of them.
flag hard section consent 2
  | The app asks for your friends' information too, and they never see this screen. Only Cancel stops it.
flag hard section feed 2
  | A message built to frighten the people it was shown to. Different people got different adverts from the same campaign.
flag soft file Desktop/survey-login.txt
  | Passwords in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read them. Use a password manager.
flag soft file Desktop/friendnet-login.txt
  | A password in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read it.

lesson
  | Read what an app asks for before you log in with a social account. A quiz does not need your birthday, your city or your friends' data.
  | Review Apps and Websites in your accounts and remove the ones you no longer use.
  | Your friends' choices can expose you too, so ask which apps your account is connected to.
